hi I'm Nick Gillespie with Reason TV and  today we're talking with Reason magazine  senior editor Radley Balko we are  looking at the three most egregious  police abuse videos that have come to  light in 2011 the first one we're  looking at is a Utah SWAT team that goes  in and tell us what they do well they  were actually investigating this guy's  roommate and they they pulled him over  realized he wasn't at the house but then  thought there was gonna be a meth supply  at the house so they went ahead and  raided the house anyway this this  gentleman was in the house he was  admitted meth addict and this guy you  know comes out thinks somebody's  breaking into his home apparently comes  out with a golf club and they opened  fire it's a it's a pretty disturbing and  shrewd and did they kill him yeah okay  next up is the Seattle Police Department  and the action happens off-camera what  are we listening to the policeman in the  car who's capturing the video what's he  talking about so he sees this a homeless  guy has a knife that was perfectly legal  under Seattle law this was how he made  some money he carved things out of wood  and sold them on the street and the cop  says tells him to drop the gun and I  think about six seconds expire from the  time he gets out of the car and  confronts the guy until he fires the  shots you also hear a woman say you know  why did you shoot him it was doing  anything it's stunning really in how  quickly things escalate from this widlar  sort of walking down the sidewalk kind  of minding those business minding his  own business until he's you know dead on  the sidewalk and in this case the cop  was was cleared there was a  investigation but there definitely won't  be any criminal charges against him and  then the final one we're looking at this  is uh some cops in Michigan who go into  a recording studio of a local heavy  metal musician who was taping a  recording with his band and tell us what  happens there so they get an anonymous  tip that this there are drugs in this  house and they find you know personal  used marijuana but  once they're inside they realize there's  always great equipment there and first  they actually start singing and the band  was recording something what we're  listening to is tape that the police  didn't realize that they were making you  hear them start talking about what of  this guy stuff that they want to take  through asset forfeiture is there a way  that we can quantify police abuse of  this sort I mean some of it is violent  you know it certainly in two of these  three cases I mean we're talking about  people being killed is this type of  police action on the rise well it's a  tough question I mean I think in terms  of we can certainly say that for example  the use of SWAT teams has increased  dramatically  we can look at asset forfeiture funds  and and how they've risen over the years  so that certainly is true in terms of  sort of non legal or illegal abuse I  guess I mean that's harder to quantify  just because you know it's hard to say  what what does and dust doesn't  constitute abuse right so you get a cop  gets captured on video beating somebody  after the Seattle cop for example this  homeless guy shooting I mean officially  that probably would not count as an  incident of police abuse because he was  cleared right so it's hard to tell  police departments are pretty reluctant  to track their own mistakes like a lot  of government agencies but you know if  you look if you talk to criminologist  actually they will say academic  criminologist will actually say that  it's it's getting better that actually  police departments are more professional  and they are more accountable and  transparent than they've been in the  past that doesn't mean it's acceptable  right it just means that it's better  than was in the 50s and 60s how has  technology changed things both in terms  of kind of citizen based technologies  they're going back to the Rodney King  beating which we only saw because a guy  was experimenting with his new video  camera but up through an age where we  have cell phones things like that but  also where the police are actually  surveilling themselves more how is that  bringing things to light and is it  helping yes I mean I think it's it's  great on on all accounts I think it's  great that police are monitoring  themselves with cameras and microphones  I think it's great that citizens can  also have their own cameras to keep  police accountable now I think it does  give  this perception that things are getting  worse because we're now being more aware  of police abuse I think traditionally  you know the police narrative was of  what happened was the official narrative  and that's the narrative the prosecutors  defer to it's what the judges deferred  to it's usually what juries believe and  now I mean there is this new very  powerful weapon that citizens have to  challenge that narrative and you know  sometimes video can be misleading it  could be taken out of context but it is  a very very powerful potent weapon and  it's I mean there are dozens and dozens  of cases where a citizen shot video is  actually shown that a police officer  lied on a police report so I think you  know it's holding police accountable  police officers accountable in those  particular cases but I also think it's  it's encouraging sort of best practices  in general because police now should go  out there business knowing that  everybody has a camera in their pocket  and I think that's a powerful incentive  Radley Balko senior editor of Reason  thanks for talking to us I'm Nick  Gillespie for Reason TV
 

